#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189124} :activity/id #uuid "939b57fa-4d38-510c-b99b-f714aa08558b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050792 :topic/id #uuid "675d7c57-7050-5b6e-8c59-6608d72d4947" :topic/math-academy-id 3872} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188300 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188007 :question/id #uuid "6cbff12b-cc20-5db4-9e64-8bb7b4c62dd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec12e1c-bc47-5ac1-a1aa-f9de136b90cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440902}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Appropriate Units of Area and Volume"} :step/id #uuid "4ce7e8a3-4577-50bf-a00f-068cd37af097" :step/math-academy-id 38276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188301 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188008 :question/id #uuid "94639d5a-c922-52f3-8e50-82d0e9a3dbad" :question/math-academy-id "e-10150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "469f0c76-3641-5a56-82bb-9ac807ff2017" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050338}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233496}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Volume With the Correct Units"} :step/id #uuid "3fc81ced-9831-5655-a85b-a4d4b3264016" :step/math-academy-id 38277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189383 :tutorial/content "**Area** measures how much space there is on a flat surface. In this lesson, we'll learn about the various measurement units of area and how they relate to units of length. \n\nConsider a square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/3f/3feaac95102a8a3014b7ab114adb97d5a0267a5c654c584bff03152801c807c2.png)\n\nTo find the area of a square, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the area of this square is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^2.\n$$\n\nThe unit of area $(\\textrm m^2)$ we used is called a **square meter.** In this case, we say that the area of our square is **one square meter.**\n\nIn previous lessons, we learned that length can also be measured in kilometers $(\\textrm{km}),$ centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}),$ and millimeters $(\\textrm{mm}).$ Each of these length units has a corresponding area unit:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{km}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{km}^2,$ or **one square kilometer**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2,$ or **one square centimeter**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{mm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{mm}^2,$ or **one square millimeter**.\n\nThe same idea applies when measuring area in customary units. For example:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^2,$ or **one square inch**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2,$ or **one square foot**." :tutorial/id #uuid "782bd823-d56b-501d-97cd-2346156da037" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "81a881d6-35a5-534b-ad14-ce4e42142162" :step/math-academy-id 38318 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189130}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188302 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188009 :question/id #uuid "5f153b4a-2706-5ff6-b0b3-65cfc29e1391" :question/math-academy-id "e-10163"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09b56d66-4e96-56de-b3fa-747cb2e3487d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050170}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440904}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Area With the Correct Units"} :step/id #uuid "6f7ec5ae-5a69-500f-9fb4-8f461d1c2564" :step/math-academy-id 38328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189242}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189385 :tutorial/content "**Volume** measures how much three-dimensional space there is inside something. \n\nConsider a cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/f0/f0006ee19c09fcf56ad032675f5f71ca8e49fc4c554a169e17bff0a09f2bbb6a.png)\n\nTo find the volume of a cube, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the volume of this cube is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^3.\n$$\n\nThe unit of volume $(\\textrm m^3)$ we used is called a **cubic meter.** In this case, we say that the volume of our cube is **one cubic meter.**\n\nAs with area, each unit of length has a corresponding volume unit. For example:\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^3,$ or **one cubic centimeter**.\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^3,$ or **one cubic inch**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ad0f170d-4023-5465-9263-b8826bdaa467" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6337 :tutorial/title "Units of Volume"} :step/id #uuid "9c654269-ba54-5587-a9b6-ba5cb053ac2d" :step/math-academy-id 38355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189389 :tutorial/content "A square is a special type of rectangle whose length and width are equal. For example, consider the square below.\n\n![](images/a9/a92233299ba67fec2a7712c88afcabeff3973dda07afdd7f91c085c4564e2aa1.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this square equals $3\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nSince a square is a special type of rectangle, we find its area by multiplying its length and width. However, since its length and width are equal, we can find its area by *squaring* its side length:\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(3\\,\\text{m})^{2}\\text{ } \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =(3\\times 3)\\times (\\text{m}\\times \\text{m}) \\\\  & =9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d7c3905-be55-58f4-8070-adad0bd8fef4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15346 :tutorial/title "Calculating Area"} :step/id #uuid "d695cf8d-6531-5468-8016-c6cf9749344f" :step/math-academy-id 633751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189433 :tutorial/content "A **cube** is a special type of rectangular solid whose length, width, and height are equal. \n\n![](images/e6/e696b4b9875052f18f394df4f0c6fb867b599a8484ac04acfca021ade9618a49.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this cube equals $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\nSince a cube is a special type of rectangular solid, we find its volume by multiplying its length, width, and height. However, since its length, width, and height are all equal, we can find its volume by *cubing* its side length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =(2\\,\\text{cm})^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\,\\text{cm})\\times (2\\,\\text{cm})\\times (2\\,\\text{cm}) \\\\  & =(2\\times 2\\times 2)\\times (\\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}) \\\\  & =8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "15a41542-a66e-5d1f-889a-ca578f48bae4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7945 :tutorial/title "Calculating Volume"} :step/id #uuid "e659ead9-3e52-5de3-86a3-60522e000640" :step/math-academy-id 45706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189123}}] :activity/title "Units of Area and Volume" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188466}]}
